2026-06-30 15:46:46,646 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-56a43dad-5830-4c3b-962f-f76128d5ca19', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20240330_HF_LC1_MF_GGO_Biotin-CCW16_Rep1.raw\n20240330_HF_LC1_MF_GGO_Biotin-CCW16_Rep2.raw\n20240330_HF_LC1_MF_GGO_Biotin-CCW16_Rep3.raw\n20240330_HF_LC1_MF_GGO_Biotin_Rep1.raw\n20240330_HF_LC1_MF_GGO_Biotin_Rep2.raw\n20240330_HF_LC1_MF_GGO_Biotin_Rep3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nStreptavidin biotin-CCW16 pulldown to determine CCW16-modified proteins in HeLa lysate\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe SUMO-targeted E3 ubiquitin ligase RNF4 plays an important role in safeguarding genome and proteome integrity. RNF4 recognizes polySUMO modified proteins and induces their proteolytic or non-proteolytic ubiquitylation. Based on the key functions of RNF4 maintaining proteome and genome stability, control of DNA damage response and DNA-protein crosslink repair, we explored its role in cancer cell survival and as a potential drug target. We found that RNF4 is overexpressed in acute myeloid leukemia and expression levels correlate with poor survival. To develop PROTACs for targeted degradation of RNF4, we aimed to exploit the recently developed cysteine-reactive RNF4 ligand CCW16, and synthesized a set of CCW16-derived degrader molecules using established VHL- and CRBN E3 ligands. To determine CCW16 specificity and binding partners, we performed a Streptavidin Biotin-CCW16 pull down of HeLa cell lysate. We detected around 2000 proteins, which showed significant increase in binding to CCW16 biotin, compared to Biotin only. Interestingly, we found proteins of the peroxiredoxin family covalently modified with CCW16 at the catalytically active cysteine residues, indicating an induction of oxidative stress.\n\n[METHODS]\nBiotin-CCW16 interactors were enriched by Streptavidin pulldown as described above. Each pulldown was performed in triplicates and in low-binding tubes (Eppendorf) and 1 mg of protein was used per pull down. For on-bead digestion, beads were again washed twice with 50 mM Tris/HCl, pH 8.5 and resuspended in sodium deoxycholate (SDC) buffer (2 % SDC, 4 mM CAA, 1 mM TCEP in 50 mM Tris/HCl, pH 8.5) and incubated for 10 min at 95 °C for alkylation and reduction of the proteins. After cooling down to room temperature, digestion buffer was added (Tris/HCl pH 8.5) including 0.5 µg Trypsin per sample and incubated overnight at 37 °C. The digestion was stopped by adding 1 % (v/v) TFA in isopropanol and peptides were loaded onto styrene-divinyl benzene reverse phasesulfonate (SDB-RPS) polymer sorbent solid phase extraction STAGE tips (Kulak et al.) for clean-up. Peptides were washed once with 1 % TFA (v/v) in isopropanol, followed by washing with 2 % ACN and 0.2 % TFA and elution in 80 % ACN/1.25 % ammonia. Peptides were dried by speed-vac at 60 °C and resuspended in 2 % ACN and 0.1 % TFA. Samples were analyzed on a Q Exactive HF coupled to an easy nLC 1200 (ThermoFisher Scientific) using a 35 cm long, 75µm ID fused-silica column packed in house with 1.9 µm C18 particles (Reprosil pur, Dr. Maisch), and kept at 50°C using an integrated column oven (Sonation). Peptides were eluted by a non-linear gradient from 4-28% acetonitrile over 45 minutes and directly sprayed into the mass-spectrometer equipped with a nanoFlex ion source (ThermoFisher Scientific). Full scan MS spectra (300-1650 m/z) were acquired in Profile mode at a resolution of 60,000 at m/z 200, a maximum injection time of 20 ms and an AGC target value of 3 x 106 charges. Up to 10 most intense peptides per full scan were isolated using a 1.4 Th window and fragmented using higher energy collisional dissociation (normalized collision energy of 27). MS/MS spectra were acquired in centroid mode with a resolution of 30,000, a maximum injection time of 54 ms and an AGC target value of 1 x 105. Single charged ions, ions with a charge state above 5 and ions with unassigned charge states were not considered for fragmentation and dynamic exclusion was set to 20s.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
